      "content": "The Court of Appeal in *Thorne v. Maritime Logistics Ltd* (2023) dismissed the appeal, holding that the doctrine of vicarious proximity from *Duncombe v. Northern Rail* (2014) does not extend to sub-contracted employees where the control test in *Smith v. Jones* (2018) is satisfied. The court relied on *Henderson v. Global Shipping* (2020) to determine that high autonomy precludes vicarious liability, while reaffirming *Miller v. Transport Corp* (2019) that proximity must be both physical and legal. Crucially, the judgment distinguishes modern logistics from earlier precedents by emphasizing that the intricate decoupling of direct supervision from operational responsibility fundamentally alters the scope of control, thereby limiting the duty of care to those with direct authority. This nuanced application ensures that the principles in *Duncombe* and *Smith* remain robust against the evolving realities of the supply chain, aligning with the foundational common law requirements for a sufficient relationship of control and foreseeability established in *Caparo Industries plc v. Dickman* (1990) and *Barnes v. Addy",
